#include "curriculum.h"

Lesson lessons[]={
{0,"Your first C program","START HERE","Every C program begins executing in main(). Use #include to access the standard library, and end statements with semicolons.","printf writes formatted text to standard output. A return value of 0 usually means the program completed successfully.","#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"Hello, C!\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}","Try changing the message and compiling with a C compiler."},
{0,"Variables & data types","ESSENTIAL","C variables have a declared type, such as int, float, double or char. Initialize variables before reading their values.","Use %d for int, %f for floating-point values, %c for char and %s for a null-terminated string when calling printf.","int score = 95;\nfloat price = 19.99f;\nchar grade = 'A';\nprintf(\"%d %c\\n\", score, grade);","const makes an object non-modifiable through that identifier."},
{0,"Conditions & loops","ESSENTIAL","if/else branches conditionally. for, while and do-while repeat code while their conditions permit.","A for loop groups initialization, condition and update; nested loops are useful for pattern-printing labs.","for (int row = 1; row <= 4; row++) {\n    for (int col = 0; col < row; col++)\n        printf(\"A\");\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n}","Practise drawing triangles with only two nested loops."},
{0,"Functions & parameters","CORE SKILL","Functions split a program into reusable pieces. Parameters passed by value are copies of the caller's values.","Returning a value and modifying an argument are different operations. Use a pointer when the function must modify the caller's object.","int square(int number) {\n    return number * number;\n}\n\nint result = square(7); // 49","Write a function to test leap years, prime numbers or palindromes."},
{0,"Arrays & strings","CORE SKILL","An array holds consecutive elements of the same type. In C, strings are char arrays terminated by '\\0'.","Always keep array bounds and string capacity in mind. Array parameters usually decay to pointers, so pass the length separately.","int values[] = {3, 9, 2, 8};\nint n = 4;\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++)\n    printf(\"%d \", values[i]);","Lab 1 covers searching, reversing, rotating and copying arrays."},
{0,"Multidimensional arrays","LAB PREP","A two-dimensional array can represent a grid with rows and columns. Nested loops visit each cell.","For a function accepting int grid[4][6], the second dimension forms part of the parameter type.","int grid[2][3] = {{1,2,3},{4,5,6}};\nfor (int r = 0; r < 2; r++)\n    for (int c = 0; c < 3; c++)\n        printf(\"%d \", grid[r][c]);","Challenge: detect a run of three equal nonzero values."},
{1,"Pointer fundamentals","HIGH PRIORITY","A pointer stores an address. & obtains the address of an object and * dereferences a valid pointer to access the pointed-to object.","The pointer itself and the value it points to are distinct. Use NULL (C) or nullptr (C++) for an intentional empty pointer.","int number = 5;\nint *ptr = &number;\n*ptr = 10;\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", number); // 10","Never dereference an uninitialized, NULL or dangling pointer."},
{1,"Pointer parameters","HIGH PRIORITY","Passing a pointer to a function lets it operate on the caller's variable instead of a local copy.","This is the central idea behind swap_ints and swap_chars in the pointer lab.","void swap_ints(int *a, int *b) {\n    int tmp = *a;\n    *a = *b;\n    *b = tmp;\n}\n\nint x = 3, y = 8;\nswap_ints(&x, &y);","In C++, references are another way to modify caller-owned objects."},
{1,"Pointer arithmetic","HIGH PRIORITY","For an int pointer p, p + 1 advances by one int element, not necessarily one byte.","Within an array, *(arr + i) accesses the same element as arr[i]. Your supplied pointer lab specifically forbids array indexing.","int a[] = {2, 4, 6};\nint *p = a;\nprintf(\"%d\\n\", *(p + 1)); // 4\n\nfor (int *it = a; it < a + 3; ++it)\n    printf(\"%d \", *it);","Only perform pointer arithmetic within an array object (or one past it)."},
{1,"Pointer-based strings","LAB PREP","C strings end with a null byte. Walk a char pointer until *p == '\\0' to measure or process a string.","Pointer exercises include strlen_ptr, count_vowels, copy_string, concat_strings and compare_strings.","int strlen_ptr(const char *s) {\n    int length = 0;\n    while (*s != '\\0') {\n        ++length;\n        ++s;\n    }\n    return length;\n}","For copies and concatenation, ensure the destination has enough capacity."},
{1,"Stack, heap & scope","EXAM FOCUS","Local automatic objects usually live until their scope ends. Dynamically allocated memory lives until explicitly released.","Returning the address of a local variable creates a dangling pointer. Dereferencing it has undefined behavior.","/* WRONG: do not return &local */\nint *bad(void) {\n    int local = 5;\n    return &local;\n}","Learn to identify dangling pointers, leaks and use-after-free bugs."},
{1,"malloc/free vs new/delete","EXAM FOCUS","C uses malloc/free for dynamic allocation; C++ can use new/delete, although modern C++ usually prefers RAII containers and smart pointers.","Match allocation with its correct deallocator: malloc with free; new with delete; new[] with delete[]. Mixing them is undefined behavior.","/* C */\nint *a = malloc(5 * sizeof *a);\nif (a != NULL) {\n    a[0] = 42;\n    free(a);\n    a = NULL;\n}\n/* include <stdlib.h> */","In C++, prefer std::vector for dynamically sized arrays where possible."},
{2,"Nodes and next pointers","HIGH PRIORITY","A singly linked list stores data in nodes. Each node points to the next node; the final node points to NULL or nullptr.","Unlike arrays, nodes need not be adjacent in memory. Traversal follows next pointers one node at a time.","typedef struct Node {\n    int data;\n    struct Node *next;\n} Node;\n\nNode first = {10, NULL};\nNode second = {20, NULL};\nfirst.next = &second;","Draw the arrows by hand before implementing insertions."},
{2,"Insert at the beginning","LAB PREP","To prepend a node, point its next field at the old head, then update head to the new node.","A pointer-to-pointer parameter lets a C function change the caller's head pointer.","void push_front(Node **head, Node *node) {\n    node->next = *head;\n    *head = node;\n}","Test an empty list as well as a non-empty list."},
{2,"Insert at the end","LAB PREP","To append, allocate a new node and find the current tail. If the list is empty, the new node becomes the head.","Using a tail pointer can make appending O(1); traversing from the head takes O(n).","void append(Node **head, Node *n) {\n    n->next = NULL;\n    if (!*head) { *head = n; return; }\n    Node *cur = *head;\n    while (cur->next) cur = cur->next;\n    cur->next = n;\n}","Remember to free all heap-allocated nodes when done."},
{2,"Traversal & removal","CORE SKILL","To traverse, advance a cursor until NULL. To remove a node, reconnect its neighbors before freeing the node.","Be careful when deleting the head or when the list is empty. Never follow next after freeing the node that owns it.","for (Node *cur = head; cur; cur = cur->next) {\n    printf(\"%d \", cur->data);\n}","A diagram makes edge cases much easier to reason about."},
{2,"Doubly linked lists","EXAM FOCUS","A doubly linked node stores next and previous links. This enables traversal in both directions.","Insertion must update the new node's links and the two neighboring nodes' links, including head/tail edge cases.","typedef struct DNode {\n    int data;\n    struct DNode *prev;\n    struct DNode *next;\n} DNode;","Your linked-list lab asks for insert-at-beginning, end and position."},
{3,"C++ classes & objects","C++","Classes package state and methods. public members form the interface; private members hide implementation details.","A constructor initializes an object. Prefer clearly defined ownership when a class holds dynamic memory.","class Counter {\n    int value = 0;\npublic:\n    void increment() { ++value; }\n    int get() const { return value; }\n};","C code cannot compile C++ class syntax; use a C++ compiler for these examples."},
{3,"Function & class templates","C++","Templates let one function or class work with multiple types without maintaining near-duplicate implementations.","template <typename T> introduces a type parameter. The compiler instantiates suitable versions when templates are used.","template <typename T>\nT maximum(T a, T b) {\n    return a > b ? a : b;\n}\n\nauto result = maximum(3, 7);","Try templating the array-sum exercise from your lecture notes."},
{3,"Operator overloading","C++","C++ lets user-defined types specify what operators like + or ++ mean for their objects.","Overloaded operators are functions. Keep behavior intuitive and do not assume every operator can be overloaded.","struct Point {\n    int x, y;\n    Point operator+(const Point& other) const {\n        return {x + other.x, y + other.y};\n    }\n};","Practise member versus non-member operators and prefix/postfix ++."},
{4,"Vectors & iterators","C++ STL","std::vector is a dynamically sized contiguous sequence. It supports indexed access, push_back and iteration.","Iterators mark positions in a container. begin() is the first element; end() is the position past the last one.","#include <vector>\nstd::vector<int> nums = {2,4,6};\nnums.push_back(8);\nfor (int x : nums) {\n    // process x\n}","Adding elements may invalidate vector iterators and references."},
{4,"std::list and splice","C++ STL","std::list is a doubly linked list: bidirectional traversal, but no random-access indexing like list[3].","push_front, push_back, sort and splice are important operations in the provided STL list lecture.","#include <list>\nstd::list<int> a = {1,2};\nstd::list<int> b = {8,9};\na.splice(a.end(), b); // moves b's nodes\n// b is now empty","Spliced-element iterators remain valid and now refer into the destination list."},
{4,"Maps, sets & adapters","C++ STL","std::map associates unique keys with values. std::set stores unique keys. std::stack and std::queue provide restricted access patterns.","Use find() when you want to inspect a key without inserting it. map[key] inserts a default value if the key is absent.","#include <map>\nstd::map<const char*, int> scores;\n// For string-value keys prefer std::string:\n// std::map<std::string, int> scores;","std::map is ordered by key; std::unordered_map is hash-based."},
{4,"Algorithms & complexity","C++ STL","Standard algorithms combine with iterators so you can reuse sorting, searching and other operations.","Choose containers based on operations: vector for indexing and locality, list for stable node insertion, map for ordered key lookup.","#include <algorithm>\n#include <vector>\nstd::vector<int> a = {5,2,9};\nstd::sort(a.begin(), a.end());","Know which operations are O(1), O(n) or O(log n)."}
};
